The problem:
It is easy to get help for a specific command (vboxmanage help command). But hard to know which command was needed
So I separated each vboxmanage command help into individual files with the following awk command.

vboxmanage |awk '/^[[:blank:]]+VBoxManage [[:alnum:]]/ {print $1, "--help", $2, ">", $2".man"}' |awk '!seen[$0]++' |zsh
The gyst:
  vboxmanage outputs the full help page
  awk pulls lines where the first word is VBoxManage preceded by one or more :blank: spaces followed by a space and another word character.
  That will be the command part. i.e. VBoxManage list
  Pass that list into awk "seen", a moniker for an array that strips out duplicate lines without sorting.
  This is needed because several of the help sections contain several of the same command with slightly different options.
    For example guestcontrol has 15 separate entries in the default help page: (VBoxManage |grep VBoxManage guestcontrol|wc)
  Pipe that output to bash to perform the operation (writing the output of VBoxManage --help <command> to a file named after the command)
  The command should be run in a folder where the output should go or written to output to that folder.
  It will only be used once so I went with cding to the folder before running.

